Joules per frame for BiSeNetV2, re-measured after fixing a process-teardown bug in
`tools/power_probe.py` that the original sitting's idle-baseline disagreement is now
traced to. Supersedes `power_rapl_bisenetv2_joules_per_frame.log` for the headline
numbers; that log's `CORRECTION` appendix carries the diagnosis of what went wrong there
and is kept for that evidence.

**NPU ~5.0x more energy-efficient than the iGPU, ~18.6x more than the CPU** (marginal
package power over a validated idle baseline, medians, throughput from a fully separate
clean pass). The dtype control and the coin-flip-latency finding both reproduce.

Desktop 2 (Ryzen 7 8700G / Phoenix XDNA1 / Radeon 780M), DESKTOP-CBL5NUA, 2026-09-10,
14:48-15:00.

WHY V2 EXISTS
=============
The original sitting reported a 41% idle-to-idle (A vs F) disagreement and responded with
a low/mean/high sensitivity range, attributing the disagreement to generic background OS
activity. Investigating that attribution (prompted by review, not by anything in the
original data alone) found the real mechanism: `tools/power_probe.py` ended its
`--command` workload with `proc.terminate()`, which on Windows only kills the `cmd.exe`
wrapper `subprocess.Popen(cmd, shell=True)` creates, not the workload underneath it.
Confirmed directly: a 5 s probe against a 60 s held workload left both processes running,
unkilled, after the tool's own 15 s `wait()` had elapsed. That means every phase's
workload could outlive its own sampling window by up to ~32 s -- long enough to bleed
through the 15 s SETTLE gap into the START of the next phase's sampling. See
`power_rapl_bisenetv2_joules_per_frame.log`'s `CORRECTION` appendix for the specific
evidence pointing at phase E contaminating phase F there, and `docs/DECISIONS.md` for the
general pitfall. Fixed by tearing the workload down with `taskkill /F /T /PID <pid>`
(kills the whole process tree), verified by repeating the orphan test and finding no
survivors.

WHY PHASE A IS EXCLUDED HERE, NOT RANGED AGAINST
=================================================
The fix did not make A and F converge -- if anything they disagree more sharply than v1's
two idle phases did, in the opposite direction (A high this time, F low):

    phase   package median   package mean   min      max
    A_idle       39.765 W        35.604 W   21.440   47.112
    F_idle       20.015 W        22.282 W   18.464   40.412

The first instinct -- report another sensitivity range -- is wrong this time, and it is
wrong for a reason that can be checked rather than assumed: **phase D (NPU, an active
compute workload) has a package median of 30.937 W, which is BELOW phase A's 39.765 W.**
An active workload cannot legitimately draw less power than true idle. That is not
consistent with A being a valid idle baseline; it is consistent with A being a
contaminated reading of a machine that had NOT settled to idle. F (20.015 W) sits below
every active phase, which is the relationship idle is supposed to have. **F is used as
the sole baseline below. A is excluded, not averaged in, and this is why.**

Per-core evidence for what contaminated A, and it lands on exactly two cores:

    core     A median (W)   F median (W)   delta
    core3         5.861          1.219     +4.64
    core5         6.674          0.408     +6.27

Roughly +11 W concentrated on two cores, absent in F -- not a package-wide effect, which
argues against a generic "background OS activity" explanation and for a specific process.
A's mean (35.604 W) sitting BELOW its own median (39.765 W) says that load was partial,
not steady across the whole 60 s window -- consistent with a short job that ended partway
through, not a sustained peer process.

Three candidates are named, none confirmed, in order of how well the timing fits:
1. **This session ran `git fetch origin` and inspected remote branches on this same host
   within roughly a minute of phase A starting**, while waiting on this study in the
   background. `git fetch` plus branch enumeration is exactly a short one-or-two-core CPU
   burst that ends on its own -- it fits the two-core, partial-duration signature above
   better than the other candidates. This is the leading hypothesis, not a proven one.
   **Methodology error, recorded so it is not repeated:** tool calls should not run on the
   measurement host while a power phase is sampling; the protocol going forward is launch,
   then touch nothing but read the output file until the study finishes.
2. A peer session's process on this machine. `Get-Process python` immediately after the
   study found nothing running, which rules out a still-live peer at check time but not
   one that had already exited by then.
3. Residual Windows process-cleanup churn from the two manual orphan-reproduction tests
   run on this host in the minutes immediately before this study started (each spawned and
   killed a `cmd.exe` / `python.exe` tree).
No hardware context was open on the NPU before or after (see raw output), so none of these
candidates involve this repo's own NPU tooling holding a context.

RESULT
======
Marginal power is phase package median minus phase F's package median (20.015 W). J/frame
is marginal watts divided by the clean-pass steady fps.

    arm                  fps    marginal W   J/frame
    CPU fp32             21.4      56.615      2.646
    iGPU (DML) fp32      81.1      57.578      0.710
    NPU xint8            76.7      10.922      0.142
    CPU xint8 (control)  11.4      44.753      3.926

    NPU vs CPU:  18.6x            NPU vs iGPU:  5.0x

Cores stay close to idle on both the iGPU and NPU phases (iGPU cores +5.3 W over F, NPU
cores +1.1 W over F) while the residual (package minus cores -- uncore, SoC, memory
controller, and for the NPU phase, the NPU) carries the rest of each phase's marginal draw
(iGPU +52.3 W residual, NPU +9.8 W residual). That is the same qualitative signature the
ResNet50 log found and BiSeNetV2 v1 said could not be drawn this sitting -- it turns out
to be visible here too, once the correct idle baseline is used. Treat this as one
supporting data point, not a second confirmed instance: it rests on a single 60 s window
per phase, not the multi-sitting agreement the ResNet50 reading had.

THE SHARPEST FINDING, UNCHANGED FROM V1: LATENCY IS A COIN FLIP, ENERGY IS NOT
===============================================================================
This sitting's own clean throughput pass reads the iGPU at 81.1 fps against the NPU's
76.7 fps -- the iGPU **1.057x faster**, the same direction and nearly the same margin as
v1's 1.056x, and still the opposite direction from `docs/BENCHMARKS.md`'s documented
1.086x NPU-over-DML. Whatever is driving that reversal is stable across both sittings on
this host, not a one-off. The energy ranking carries no such ambiguity: the NPU is 5.0x
more efficient than the iGPU regardless of which sitting's latency number is believed.

THE DTYPE CONTROL REPRODUCES
=============================
CPU on the same xint8 artifact is slower (11.4 vs 21.4 fps) and 1.48x worse on energy
(3.926 vs 2.646 J/frame) than CPU fp32 -- the same pessimization found on ResNet50 and in
v1 of this study, now confirmed against a clean baseline rather than a ranged one.

METHODOLOGY
===========
Six power phases (60 s sampled, 15 s settle after each), then a fully separate throughput
pass (12 one-second samples per arm, first dropped, 8 s settle between arms) -- the same
structure v1 used, with `tools/power_probe.py`'s teardown fixed and `HOLD` tightened to
`DUR + 5` (65 s) so each workload's own timer expires inside the settle gap even if the
tree-kill ever failed. No throughput figure here shows the halving signature the ResNet50
study found and retracted: the untimed post-compile NPU check read 77.2 fps and the clean
pass read 76.7 fps, consistent rather than roughly half of each other.

Two gaps, both process rather than data problems, recorded so the template is fixed
before the next model in this series:
- This script's copy of the driver dropped the ResNet50 script's `peer()` PID snapshot
  (a `Get-Process` check bracketing the run). Restore it in the next model's script --
  it would have either confirmed or ruled out candidate 2 above directly.
- Tool calls ran on this host during phase A (see above). Do not repeat.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT ESTABLISH
=============================
- **The excluded-baseline argument rests on one clean idle sample (F), not a replicated
  one.** The physical-impossibility check (an active phase reading below the candidate
  baseline) is a strong reason to exclude A, but F itself has not been cross-checked
  against a second independent idle sitting the way ResNet50's 0.8% A/F agreement served
  as its own cross-check. A third sitting with the process-hygiene fixes above would
  strengthen this further; not done here.
- **One model, batch 1, single stream**, same scope limits as every log in this series.
- **Not sustained thermal steady state** -- 60 s windows.
- **Throughput and power come from separate windows**, minutes apart.
- **The NPU residual is an upper bound**, not an isolate.
- **No accuracy axis** -- see `power_rapl_bisenetv2_joules_per_frame.log` and
  `docs/BENCHMARKS.md` for BiSeNetV2's known fixed-point fidelity issue; this log compares
  energy at fixed work, not at fixed accuracy.

